r own words Maryland for  instance they say come to our Honors  Program they're competing against  private schools for those students and  in it you'll take small classes taught  by full faculty as opposed to regular  Maryland students are gonna take huge  lecture classes often taught by graduate  students and all of this so in their own  words I was able to prove the neglect of  undergraduate education I remember the  University of Oregon said our Honors  Program will teach you such fundamental  skills as reading and writing and I  thought wait a second  you mean the regular classes won't teach  those fundamental skills I guess that's  what they're saying that's in their own  words so in that sense the honors  programs helped me now what I actually  think about them as a strong advocate of  public universities I have always  refused to teach in the Honors Program  at Indiana that seems to me a kind of  easy way out a lot of faculty love it  because they can actually do some of  their research with the honors students  but it's a much greater challenge to me  to teach regular undergraduates as I was  at Purdue Purdue didn't have an Honors  Program at that point and nor did most  schools and you know just I don't feel  they belong in public universities I  think it's a way of schools avoiding  their problems with undergraduate  education it's maybe not be a fair  question but do your books sell they do  I seem to there seems to be an audience  for that and I notice on amazon.com this  books been in triple digits which is  quite good and I did a book about the  history of Notre Dame football that's  old exceptionally well and yeah they so  quickly in your research and you say he  went to 40 different schools in your  research right who's who which  university was the most friendly and  said come on in you're gonna look at  everything  ah actually Stanford University wasn't  it's a private school Notre Dame was  historically people have a terrific  misconception about a Notre Dame that  they're hostile to the outside world  quite the opposite they are totally  professional they let me go wherever I  want and talk to whoever I wanted no  holds barred who is the least friendly  ah probably well it's hard to say there  are schools that just said don't come  here Syracuse University yeah was not  interested in me going there I did I had  some web surveys I said don't come they  said no they didn't say don't come they  just from there you know we're very busy  you know we're doing our own research on  these topics and you know we'll get back  to you a sort of thing in the brief time  we have left that how much of this do  you blame on somebody like CBS and an 11  year contract with the National  Collegiate Athletic Association and how  much is it worth six billion not six  million six billion dollars I blame a  lot well they're doing you know they're  in the TV business  I blame universities and the NCAA for  taking the money in the sense and not  admitting this is not a that's not six  billion as a contribution to higher  education that's payment for a product  and the product the schools have to turn  out is in this case college basketball  played at the highest possible level  which means putting players in these  very intensive training programs making  it difficult for them to get a  meaningful education and also pretending  the NCAA in schools have this whole  propaganda machine about student  athletes and they show you that thing at  halftime and all of that and pretending  that this is a wonderful system the six  billion  helps higher education enormous ly what  it does is it distorts public opinion  I've I do a certain amount of speaking  at Kiwanis Clubs and Rotary clubs and  such and meet ordinary people and they  truly they see those numbers and the  bold numbers and they truly believe that  the money from college sports funds  other parts of the universities the Bob  night fans constantly said don't I  realize my salary as an English  professor is paid by the athletic  department from its profits that is  totally false so in a sense it hurts the  universities in all kinds of ways how  many of these coaches make a million  dollars a year oh gee at this point I'd  say about 40 are approaching them in  basketball and football pays for that  essentially the University now part of  it is the deals with the shoe contracts  and such but Nike does not pay a coach  because they love some middle-aged fat  coach they want that team to win and the  Nike swoosh is very visible on TV they  want fans to look in and see the players  are wearing their product so when they  pay a coach hundreds of thousands of  dollars essentially that's money that  should go to the school but doesn't and  goes to the coach so it's a very  distorted system but unfortunately  increasing numbers of faculty look at  that and say hey I should be an  entrepreneur like the coach will we see  you back in the Indiana University  English classroom I'm planning to be  back in January although the problem is  the night thing doesn't seem to end the  fans don't give up there you look at  their web sites and there's this fantasy  that the trustees are gonna firebrand or  a judge will intervene and do it and  bring Bob Knight back and until they  actually give up I believe until he is  coaching at another school I you cannot  move past them who you have a champion  out there now somebody in public  education who is leading to change  things well there's a group of faculty  that's organized that I'm a member of  called naff card  National Alliance for collegiate  athletic reform that has a number of  prominent faculty members across the  country that we're gonna we spoke at the  night Commission hearings on October  18th in Washington and you know there  that's funded by the knight-ridder  Foundation and they investigated college  sports 10 years ago father Hesburgh of  Notre Dame and Bill Friday Chancellor of  the Carroll North Carolina system our  co-chairs  and they're interested in our ideas now  whether it can have an effect or not  remains to be seen  our guest has been Mary Sperber and he  is the author of this book beer and  circus how big-time college sports is  crippling undergraduate education thank  you very much thank you beer and circus  is published by Henry Holton company on  the web at Henry Holt com  next week on encore booknotes Phillip  Tubman who looks at space espionage in  secret Empire for
 

